SUMMARY:ROM:  Weston International Award--Evening with Hanif Abdurraqib ($
 20)
DESCRIPTION:TORONTO ARTS & CULTURE\n{ This Author EVENT is Sold-Out }\n\nA
 lways a HOT ticket--an important (international) AUTHOR Talk. At the ROM.\
 n\n($15-$20 only!!)\n\nAmerican author **Hanif Abdurraqib has won the 2026
  Weston International Award. $75\,000**\n\n**Overview--**\n\nThe evening w
 ill feature **Hanif Abdurraquib** delivering a talk and having an in-depth
  conversation with **CBC ARTS.**\n\n\\*\\*\\*Join us on Monday\, September
  14 at the Royal Ontario Museum\, for an evening with the 2026 Weston Inte
 rnational Award winner\, hosted by Ali Hassan of CBC’s Canada Reads.\n\n
 **Hanif Abdurraqib is an American memoirist and poet known for his distinc
 tive style of cultural and artistic criticism through the lens of music\,*
 * **sport\, and contemporary American issues**. In a creative and wide-ran
 ging talk\, he will bring his unique and rare intellect to the role of non
 fiction in our society\, and his relationship to it as someone from an unc
 onventional background.\n\nHe’ll then sit down for wide-ranging and free
 -wheeling conversation with a member of the award jury and author Tessa Mc
 Watt. Jurors said his “work sings and stings and brings truths\, both pe
 rsonal and communal\, as he voices culture and its complexities with bold\
 , compassionate lyricism and a real sense of love\,” and that is what he
  will bring to the stage.\n\n***The $75\,000 prize is a companion to the e
 xisting Hilary Weston Writers' Trust Prize for Nonfiction\, which is award
 ed annually to a Canadian author for a single work of nonfiction.***\n\nTh
 e Weston International Award recognizes the career achievement of an inter
 national author for a body of nonfiction work. Eligible international auth
 ors must have published at least three books of outstanding literary merit
 \, in the genre of nonfiction\, that are written in English or else widely
  available in translation.\n\n**Ticket link here:**\n\nhttps://www.eventbr
 ite.ca/e/the-weston-international-award-presents-an-evening-with-hanif-abd
 urraqib-tickets-1983560042483?aff=oddtdtcreator\n\n**Recent interview on C
 BC:**\n\nhttps://www.cbc.ca/books/american-author-hanif-abdurraqib-wins-75
 k-weston-international-award-for-career-achievement-in-nonfiction-9.723579
 6\n\nSEE you there.
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/toronto-arts-and-culture/events/31548
 8080/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260630T183839Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260630T183839Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b737f564ef0d72c0eb21"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Patrick Radden Keefe: London Falling
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260914T200000
UID:6a85b737f564ef0d72c0eb21@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents acclaimed investigative journalist Patrick Radden
  Keefe in conversation with Rachel Giese at the Toronto Reference Library'
 s Appel Salon.\nFrom the bestselling author of Empire of Pain and Say Noth
 ing\, Patrick Radden Keefe\, comes\nLondon Falling: A Mysterious Death in 
 a Gilded City and a Family's Search for Truth\n\, a gripping investigation
  into extreme wealth\, dark truths\, power\, identity and inequality.\nThr
 ough the investigation of teenager Zac Brettler’s fall to death from a l
 uxury riverside apartment\, Keefe deftly fills-in the gaps of this tragic 
 story with the skill of an investigative journalist. By exploring the soci
 etal and familial roots behind Brettler’s death\, Keefe transforms a tru
 e-crime mystery into a meditation on modern parenting\, unresolvable grief
 \, and the corrosive effects of isolation in a city where wealth means eve
 rything.\nWhat does London's underworld of dirty money and untouchable pow
 er reveal about the wider forces reshaping cities everywhere? And how do f
 amilies rebuild their understanding of someone they loved after learning t
 hey never fully knew them at all?\nJoin one of the most acclaimed nonficti
 on writers working today\, in conversation with journalist and critic Rach
 el Giese\, for a riveting exploration of truth\, deception\, and the hidde
 n costs of a gilded world.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books availabl
 e for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nPatrick Radden Keefe\nRache
 l Giese\nRead more:\nLondon Falling\nSay Nothing\nEmpire of Pain\n***\nPLA
 N YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference L
 ibrary (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event st
 arts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe ov
 ersell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people have an o
 pportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is
  reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available f
 or purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to acce
 ssibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to request 
 accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or
  416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is
  part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and internati
 onal authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books 
 and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informat
 ion on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Publ
 ic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information wil
 l only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluatio
 n. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the p
 rogram. Questions about how the library handles your information can be di
 rected to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge
  Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b737f564ef0d72c0eb21
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
