BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Civic Tech Toronto": "https://guild.host/events/civic-meetup-560-unnecessary-0wipnh"}
X-SOURCE-ID:guildhost_civic_tech
SUMMARY:Civic Meetup #560: Unnecessary and Disproportionate: Bill C-22 and
  Lawful Access
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T210000
UID:21e8e473363bec97ff0f58820ef057af@guild.host
DESCRIPTION:New technologies and organizations’ practices are regularly 
 placing pressures and challenges on our privacy and transparency rights. T
 his month\, Civic Tech Toronto and the Information and Privacy Commissione
 r of Ontario are highlighting the work to defend\, enforce\, explain and u
 pdate these rights. To see all September events\, check our events listing
  on Guild.&#x20\;\n\n‎‎\n\nTopic: Unnecessary and Disproportionate: Bi
 ll C-22 and Lawful Access\n\nThis talk will present on a recent joint brie
 f published by the Citizen Lab and the Canadian Civil Liberties Associatio
 n\, which analyzed the privacy and human rights implications of Bill C-22:
  An Act respecting lawful access.\n\nBrief: "(Un)forced Errors: An Analysi
 s of Proposed Surveillance Expansion under Bill C-22\, An Act respecting l
 awful access" by Cynthia Khoo\, Tamir Israel\, and Kate Robertson.\n\n‎
 ‎\n\nSpeaker: Cynthia Khoo \\[in-person]\n\nCynthia Khoo is a technology
  and human rights lawyer based in Toronto\, and a Senior Fellow at the Cit
 izen Lab. She has developed and provides\, through her legal practice and 
 research\, expertise specialized in how digital technologies impact the eq
 uality\, privacy\, and free expression rights of historically marginalized
  groups. / Website: tekhnoslaw.ca / Bluesky: cyn-k.bsky.social\n\n‎\n\nA
 genda:\n\n7:00-7:20 Welcome and Introductions\n\n7:20-7:50 Presentation an
 d Q\\&A/discussion\n\n7:50-9:00 Breakout groups\n\n‎\n\nCode of Conduct:
  https://civictech.ca/about-us\n\n‎\n\nCheck in with us on the Civic Tec
 h Toronto Slack: https://link.civictech.ca/chat\n\n‎\n\nAbout Us:\n\nOur
  weekly civic meetups bring together Torontonians (designers\, coders\, ur
 ban planners\, government staff\, mappers\, policy-makers\, students\, com
 munications strategists\, community organizers\, and more) who share an in
 terest in making Toronto more responsive\, prosperous\, sustainable\, and 
 equitable through design\, tech\, and data.\n\nCome and be part of it!\n\n
 For more info: https://civictech.ca\n\n\\--\n\nInterested in speaking at a
  future event\, or know someone who'd be a great speaker? We'd love to hea
 r from you:\n\nhttps://link.civictech.ca/speaker-suggestions\n\nHave a spa
 ce that could host up to 80 civic-minded folks for an evening? Consider re
 commending your venue:\n\nhttps://link.civictech.ca/venue-suggestions\n\nV
 enue Entrances:\n\nFrom Bloor St or subway concourse\, or from Yonge St (j
 ust beside Jack Astor's)\n\nBloor-01\n\nBloor-02\n\nYonge\n
LOCATION:Online: https://link.civictech.ca/zoom / Information and Privacy 
 Commissioner of Ontario
