X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardinermuseum": "https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/blue-willow-in-the-clouds-the-architectural-reuse-of-willow-ware-in-19th-century-india/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardinermuseum
X-SOURCE:Gardiner Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260917T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260917T130000
DTSTAMP:20260811T182924Z
CREATED:20260811T182824Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260811T182924Z
UID:146639-1789646400-1789650000@www.gardinermuseum.on.ca
SUMMARY:Blue Willow in the Clouds: The Architectural Reuse of Willow Ware in 19th-Century India
DESCRIPTION:Blue Willow in the Clouds: The Architectural Reuse of Willow Ware in 19th-Century India            \n                                    \n                                \n                        \n                                                \n                                                        Get Tickets                        \n                                            \n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n			\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Thursday September 17\, 2026\n12:00 pm – 1:00 pm ET\nOnline via Zoom \nPart of the Gardiner Signature Lecture Series\nThe Robert and Marian Cumming Lecture \nSpeaker: Heeryoon Shin\, Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture\, Bard College \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    The popular Willow pattern seen on Staffordshire transferware found a new life as ornamentation in palace interiors in 19th-century India. Set into walls in their original form or broken into flat\, rectilinear pieces\, Blue Willow plates and their luminous blue-and-white surface effectively framed gods and kings\, creating a multisensory space. \nTaking the late 19th-century sites of Juna Mahal in Dungarpur and Junagadh Fort in Bikaner as points of departure\, this online talk will explore how the design and materiality of Willow ware and its display acquired new meaning and purpose in Indian palaces. \nEvoking the tradition of tiled ornamentation and the display of ceramics in the chini khana (“China room”) in India\, while also referencing European porcelain rooms\, the transferware-covered walls reveal the complex cultural negotiations and material and political aspirations of 19th-century India. By examining the Indian reuse of Willow ware\, which has been largely studied in terms of Western responses to Chinese export porcelain\, this talk complicates the conventional narrative of West looking East and instead highlights the nonlinear and multidirectional flows of ceramic culture. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n			\n\n        \n          \n          \n                        \n               \n                General : PWYC starting at $5\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Students : Free\n                                        \n                      \n\n                    \n          \n            \n  \n            \n                                Get Tickets\n              \n                        \n           \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n          \n          \n        \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n  \n    \n      \n\n              \n          \n                          \n                \n              \n                      \n        \n          \n          \n      \n      \n        \n        \n        Junagadh Fort\nBikaner\, India\nPhoto: Radiokafka \n \n        \n      \n    \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Speaker\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Heeryoon Shin \n                    \n                \n                \n                    Heeryoon Shin is Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture at Bard College. Her current book project explores architectural revival\, mobility\, and cross-cultural exchange in eighteenth-century India through the lens of temple architecture in the pilgrimage city of Banaras. She is also developing a second project on the global circulation of blue-and-white ceramics and their interaction with local production and use in South Asia. \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/blue-willow-in-the-clouds-the-architectural-reuse-of-willow-ware-in-19th-century-india/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Programs & Events
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/wp-content/uploads/Cumming-Lecture-2026.jpg
GEO:43.6679938;-79.3935128
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Gardiner Museum 111 Queen's Park Toronto ON M5S 2C7 Canada;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=111 Queen's Park:geo:-79.3935128,43.6679938
END:VEVENT
