BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c0ee60ccaf01c022533f"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:A Chorus of Connection ft. Choir! Choir! Choir!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T200000
UID:6a95c0ee60ccaf01c022533f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The evening will explore the theme of harmony and finding the 
 collective beauty in individual voices.\nMake the most of your\nConnect IR
 L\nexperience by coming before or staying after the featured event. Grab a
  drink or snack at the bar. Try out one of the engagement activities avail
 able\, including this event's\nCon\nversation\nStation\n:\nMusic\nCaf\ne\n
 \, where you can take a moment or two to connect with a stranger through t
 he topic of music.\n*****\nJoin us\nin\nthe Appel Salon for a Chorus of Co
 nnection\nwith\nChoir! Choir! Choir! founders\, Daveed\nGoldman\nand\nNobu
  Adilman\n\,\nin performance and conversation with Craig Pike and host Ela
 min Abdelmahmoud\n.\nFor one evening\, the Appel Salon becomes a choir. Da
 veed Goldman and Nobu Adilman will teach a song to a room full of people w
 ho have never sung together\, and you'll hear a crowd turn into something 
 that sounds and feels like a community. Between songs\, Goldman and Adilma
 n will be joined by That Choir Artistic Director Craig Pike to talk about 
 why choirs matter\, and what it tells us about the kinds of gatherings our
  city needs more of.\nWhat is it about singing together that cuts through 
 isolation so effectively? How does harmonizing with people we've never met
  create bonds that outlast the song? And what can an institution like the 
 public library learn from a model built entirely on radical\, no-barrier p
 articipation.\nCome for an evening where strangers become a choir\, and a 
 choir becomes community.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nChoir! Choir! Choi
 r!\nCraig Pike\nElamin Abdelmahmoud\n***\nWarm-up Voices Workshop | 5:30 -
  6:30 pm\nThis workshop will explore music in a new way while you connect
  with others in the room (no musical background or experience is necessary
 ). Registration will open for this very soon.\nThe Music Caf\né!\n| 5:30 
 – 6:30 pm & 8:00 – 9:00 pm\nPull up a chair at the Music Café\, where
  the conversation starters are about the songs that made you cry\, the alb
 um that got you through a breakup or the lyric you still can't get out of 
 your head. Prompt cards on artists\, genres\, memories and moods will be a
 vailable. This is a low-stakes way to bond with a stranger over shared tas
 te (or gloriously different ones). Take a seat\, swap a song story\, and l
 eave with a new favourite recommendation.\nConnect IRL\nis not your averag
 e series of events\, workshops and activities\, but a real chance to conne
 ct with other Torontonians in meaningful and sometimes unexpected ways. Th
 rough conversation stations meant to spark one-on-one conversations\, or h
 ands-on engagement activities and workshops before the featured event\, pa
 rticipants can explore the value of connection and community.\n***\nPLAN Y
 OUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Libr
 ary (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 5:30pm\, Onstage eve
 nt starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\n
 We oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people have
  an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capaci
 ty is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine availa
 ble for purchase (debit/credit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to
  accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to req
 uest accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.
 ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contact
 ed to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how t
 he library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at
  answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c0ee60ccaf01c022533f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a832b259d8416003f69c765"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Royal Conservatory of Music & Telus Centre
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T200000
UID:6a832b259d8416003f69c765@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This beautiful 1880s Toronto Baptist College building\, now th
 e Royal Conservatory of Music\, was sensitively restored in the early 21st
  century. The catalyst was the addition of the innovative Telus Centre for
  Performance and Learning. Through dozens of photographs\, architectural h
 istorian Marta O'Brien will discuss the exteriors and interiors of this wo
 nderful complex dedicated to music education and performance.\nDoors open 
 at 6:30pm\nPresented in collaboration with North Toronto Historical Societ
 y.
LOCATION:Northern District\, Room 200
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a832b259d8416003f69c765
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a997fb029a7dd0d6748c43e"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Surviving Dementia Care: The Realities of Family Caregiving (Autho
 r Talk)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260923T200000
UID:6a997fb029a7dd0d6748c43e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Surviving Dementia Care\nis a compassionate and insightful gui
 de for anyone navigating the challenging journey of caring for a loved one
  with dementia. Drawing on research from information studies\, literary st
 udies\, and interviews with family caregivers\, this book reimagines demen
 tia care – not as a set of skills to master\, but as a deeply human expe
 rience to endure and understand.\n​Author and Professor Emeritus D. Gran
 t Campbell will address the experiences of families who are caring for a 
 loved one who is living with dementia\, including the various points of st
 ress\, as well as the various points of joy\, and ways in which caregivers
  learn to survive the experience. Major topics include understanding the d
 iagnosis\, communicating with the loved one\, choosing an assisted living 
 facility\, and using music and the arts as a means of connection.\nInforma
 tion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collecte
 d by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 
 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and
  program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the l
 ibrary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nan
 swerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a997fb029a7dd0d6748c43e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
