SUMMARY:Memory and Aging
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T150000
UID:6aa3130fa5dbde002aa3a2bf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Psychologist Dr. Nicole Anderson discusses kinds of cognitive 
 functions change as a part of healthy aging\, and provides examples of nor
 mal memory slips\, and not so normal memory slips? This presentation discu
 sses factors affecting cognition\, including medical factors\, and everyda
 y lifestyle factors\, then provides a number of memory strategies that peo
 ple can use to make their memory function better.\nDr. Anderson is the Sen
 ior Scientist at the Rotman Research Institute (Baycrest) and  Professor\,
  Psychology and Psychiatry (University of Toronto). She is the author of "
 \nLiving with mild cognitive impairment : a guide to maximizing brain heal
 th and reducing risk of dementia"\,\nOnline registration is\nrecommended\n
 Drop-in space will be available until capacity is reached.\nInformation Co
 llection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by th
 e Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\,
  20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and progra
 m registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library 
 handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerlin
 e@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Eglinton Square\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa3130fa5dbde002aa3a2bf
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085da"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Financial Workshops for Seniors
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T160000
UID:6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085da@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Presented by John Stapleton from Open Policy Ontario\, learn m
 ore about government programs\, benefits and eligibility criteria for seni
 ors. These sessions are designed for seniors with low income and those who
  advise them.\nThursday Oct 1: What Do Seniors Get?\nLearn about benefits
  for older adults living in Ontario\, including Canada Pension Plan (CPP)\
 , Old Age Security (OAS)\, Guaranteed Income Supplement (GIS)\, and Guaran
 teed Annual Income Supplement (GAINS).\nThursday Oct 15: Understanding the
  Personal Income Tax System.\nWhat is a taxable income and how can tax cre
 dits\, deductions\, entitlements and exemptions help to reduce taxable inc
 ome.\nThursday Oct 29: Protecting Your Retirement Income.\nLearn how to re
 duce claw backs to GIS and GAINS payments if receiving additional income 
 from employment\, a private pension\, or a Registered Retirement Savings P
 lan (RRSP).\nThursday Nov 12: Coming to Canada and Leaving Canada.\nLearn 
 about eligible benefits for those that resided in Canada for fewer than 4
 0 years between the ages of 18 and 65\, or for those planning on emigratin
 g from Canada after retiring.\nThursday Nov 26: Tax Credits and Benefits f
 or Seniors and People with Disabilities.\nLearn about other tax credits an
 d benefits for seniors\, people with disabilities\, and caregivers\, inclu
 ding the disability tax credit\, the Canada caregiver credit\, and the hom
 e accessibility tax credit.\nRegistration not required. Drop-in welcome.
LOCATION:Queen/Saulter
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085da
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
