BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b4dd36204e08fbb58248"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Does Canada Need a National Portrait Gallery?
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261006T200000
UID:6a85b4dd36204e08fbb58248@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join four leading voices to imagine what could be one of Canad
 a’s most significant cultural institutions yet to be built.\nCanadians a
 re experiencing a renewed sense of who we are and why it matters to be us 
 – is now the time to respond to decades of demand for a National Portrai
 t Gallery?\nWhile the majority of countries include portraits of their 'gr
 eat and good' in their history museums\, art galleries\, archives and monu
 ments\; some countries have chosen a more democratic path – creating sta
 nd-alone National Institutions in their capital cities that celebrate not 
 only the political and military greats\, but also the change-makers in eve
 ry walk of life\, age\, ethnicity and talent. Canada has no institutional 
 equivalent – is this a quiet statement about our national character and 
 preference for practical leadership over hero-worship\, or a decentralized
  sense of identity that resists a single national narrative? Or is it simp
 ly an overlooked institutional gap that deserves to be filled?\nAcclaimed 
 UK-based architect Jamie Fobert\, celebrated Indigenous curator and histor
 ian Tim Johnson\, and writer and activist Sarah Lazarovic join moderator G
 ail Lord for a wide-ranging conversation about what such an institution co
 uld mean for the country.\nTogether they’ll examine the case for a Natio
 nal Portrait Gallery: what it might contain\, how it could meaningfully in
 clude and center Indigenous voices and communities\, where it might be loc
 ated\, and how it could be funded and supported – including whether the 
 political will currently exists to make it a reality. The panel will also 
 consider what a distinctly Canadian portrait gallery might look like in co
 ntrast to its counterparts in the United States\, United Kingdom\, and Aus
 tralia\, and what unique story it could tell about the nation and its peop
 le.\nQ&A to follow.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nJamie Fobert\nTim Johns
 on\nSarah Lazarovic\nGail Lord\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and
  Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd fl
 oor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree regis
 tration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that
  the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guar
 anteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, fir
 st served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n
 Accessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have
  a disability and would like to request accommodation for this event\, ple
 ase contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks
  prior to the event.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Seri
 es\n\, where we host local and international authors\, artists and thinker
 s in conversation about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation C
 ollection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by t
 he Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\
 , 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and progr
 am registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to
  fill out a brief survey following the program. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answ
 erline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b4dd36204e08fbb58248
