BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardinermuseum": "https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-caribbean-canadian-ceramics/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardinermuseum
X-SOURCE:Gardiner Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261009T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20270425T235959
DTSTAMP:20260908T220259Z
CREATED:20260724T133925Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260908T220259Z
UID:145982-1791504000-1808697599@www.gardinermuseum.on.ca
SUMMARY:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics
DESCRIPTION:After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics                                                                           \n        \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n	\n\n	\n\n\n			\nEvents\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    \n\nOctober 9\, 2026 – April 25\, 2027\nExhibition Hall. Third Floor\nIncluded with admission. Free for Gardiner Members\n\n\n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n  Get Tickets\n\n\n\n  Become a Member\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    The Gardiner Museum\, Black Artists’ Networks in Dialogue (BAND)\, and the Museum of the African Diaspora (MoAD) present a landmark curatorial partnership exploring contemporary ceramic practices rooted in Caribbean histories\, memory\, and cultural transformation. \nPresented across two exhibitions and two cities—Toronto and San Francisco—this collaboration brings together artists whose work examines migration\, ornament\, labour\, ancestry\, and the enduring impact of Caribbean cultural traditions across generations and geographies. \nAt the Gardiner Museum\, After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics considers how artists of the Caribbean diaspora are embracing the medium of ceramics to explore themes of knowledge transfer and the liberatory potential of the clandestine. Through existing and new work by Caribbean Canadian artists PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood\, the exhibition approaches questions of identity\, belonging\, memory\, and cultural continuity through the language of clay. \nAt MoAD\, After the Island: Ornament and Ceramic Practice in the Jamaican Diaspora focuses on artists whose practices engage Jamaica as memory\, inheritance\, orientation\, and cultural touchstone. The exhibition examines how ornament functions as a carrier of history\, labour\, ancestral knowledge\, and relation across time and distance. \nTogether\, After the Island: Caribbean Canadian Ceramics and After the Island: Ornament and Ceramic Practice in the Jamaican Diaspora offer a timely exploration of migration\, memory\, and cultural continuity\, demonstrating how artists working with clay continue to create new forms of relation between past and present\, homeland and diaspora\, history and imagination. \nThroughout both presentations\, vessels\, decorative forms\, sculptural installations\, and ceramic surfaces become sites where histories are preserved\, challenged\, reimagined\, and carried forward. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n                      \n                Upcoming Programs\n            \n            \n                        \n            \n                                     \n                    \n                                                \n                    \n                                            \n              \n                                            \n        \n        \n        \n        \n                        \n                        \n        \n               \n        \n                    \n                \n                        \n                \n            \n                                    \n                After the Island: Public Opening\n                \n            \n                         \n                Oct 8 2026 @ 7:30 pm\n                                                    \n                Join the artists and curators for evening of art\, music\, and community. Free with registration!\n            \n                        \n                        \n                Learn more\n            \n                    \n                \n                                \n        \n               \n        \n                    \n                \n                        \n                \n            \n                                    \n                After the Island: Artist Panel\n                \n            \n                         \n                Oct 9 2026 @ 6:00 pm\n                                                    \n                Curators Joséphine Denis and Dr. Sequoia Miller will moderate a panel featuring artists PJ Anderson\, Eddy Firmin\, Manuel Mathieu\, and Sharon Norwood\, followed by a Q&A.\n            \n                        \n                        \n                Learn more\n            \n                    \n                \n                                \n    \n                    \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Exhibition Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Presenting Sponsors\n                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Supporting Partners\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Government Support\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n                    \n                Project Partner\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Media Partners\n                \n                                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n              \n                    \n                Thanks To\n                \n                                \n                            \n            \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Image: Eddy Firmin\, Sans signe\, 2023\, Ceramic\, wood\, fabric\, straw\, Variable dimensions. Photo: Michael Patten \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/after-the-island-caribbean-canadian-ceramics/
LOCATION:Gardiner Museum\, 111 Queen's Park\, Toronto\, ON\, M5S 2C7\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/wp-content/uploads/After-the-Island-Thumbnail-retouched.jpg
GEO:43.6679938;-79.3935128
X-APPLE-STRUCTURED-LOCATION;VALUE=URI;X-ADDRESS=Gardiner Museum 111 Queen's Park Toronto ON M5S 2C7 Canada;X-APPLE-RADIUS=500;X-TITLE=111 Queen's Park:geo:-79.3935128,43.6679938
END:VEVENT
