SUMMARY:The Information Battle: Elections\, Journalism and Misinformation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T193000
UID:6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:As elections take place in an increasingly complicated online 
 world\, voters are exposed to a huge mix of news\, social media posts\, AI
 -generated content\, and false information spread by organized groups. New
  digital technologies have made it easier for people to learn about issues
  and take part in civic life\, but they have also raised concerns about tr
 ust\, fair elections\, and respectful public discussion.\nThis panel bring
 s together experts in misinformation\, journalism\, and election administr
 ation to explore the challenges and opportunities facing democracy today. 
 How are AI and new technologies changing the way people get information? W
 hat do these changes mean for voters\, journalists\, and election official
 s? And what can be done to build public trust in elections while helping p
 eople stay informed and engaged in public life?\nFeaturing\nNick Hune-Brow
 n\n\, Journalist and Author\nAward-winning magazine writer and Executive E
 ditor at The Local\nValerie Kindarji\nPolitical scientist at the Universit
 y of Toronto focused on the impacts of disinformation on democracies in Eu
 rope and North America and Program Manager for the Digital Transparency Re
 search Excellence Cluster (DTC) at the University of British Columbia\nTob
 ias Novogrodsky\nDeputy City Clerk\, Election Services\, City of Toronto\n
 Toronto's municipal election takes place on October 26\, 2026. Check out t
 he\nKnowYourVote.TO\nwebsite and the\nCelebrate Democracy\npage to learn m
 ore about the upcoming election\, access important voter resources\, and d
 iscover activities and interactive library programs for all ages happening
  at a branch near you.\nThis initiative is made possible by donations to t
 he Toronto Public Library Foundation and is generously supported by the Mc
 Connell Foundation.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal informat
 ion on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Publ
 ic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information wil
 l only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluatio
 n. Questions about how the library handles your information can be directe
 d to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge S
 treet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9dc0591c197d11325dc656
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Teens
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Young Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_young_adults
SUMMARY:AI for Job Hunting: Build Your Own System
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T200000
UID:6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Job hunting is a job in itself: applications\, deadlines\, int
 erviews\, follow-ups. What if you could build your own AI-powered system t
 o help you manage it?\nIn this hands-on class\, you'll explore a job-hunti
 ng dashboard built with AI: how it tracks applications\, sets automatic al
 erts for postings that match you\, and practices interviews with you throu
 gh role-play. You'll leave with the templates to build your own. We'll als
 o talk about how employers screen applications\, and how it can make thing
 s unfair. Come explore a tool that works for you\, not the other way aroun
 d.\nPrerequisites: Mouse\, keyboarding and internet skills. Computers ar
 e provided. No coding experience required.\nRegistration is required and 
 will start September 9\, 2026 at 9:00 AM.\nNote: Your spot will not be gua
 ranteed after 5 minutes of the start of the program.\nAbout the AI Tools U
 sed in This Program\nThis program includes activities that use agentic AI 
 tools (listed below) on library laptops.\nYou do not need to create your o
 wn account for this program.\nParticipants are encouraged to follow the Re
 sident’s guidance throughout sessions\, which include best practices on 
 using AI.\nWe encourage you to review the privacy statements of each tool 
 prior to attending this program:\nClaude Code\nLearn more about the tool\n
 Review privacy policy\nClaude Cowork\nLearn more about the tool\nReview pr
 ivacy policy\nGoogle Anti-Gravity\nLearn more about the tool\nReview priva
 cy policy\nGoogle AI Studio\nLearn more about the tool\nReview privacy pol
 icy\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form
  is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act
 \, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used f
 or event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions abo
 ut how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline 
 staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto 
 ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, 2nd Floor eLearning Lab
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a9b4e2d1c197d11325d6f1d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Young Adults
END:VEVENT
