SUMMARY:Birds and Our Christmas Traditions
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T150000
UID:6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Birds are woven into the fabric of Christmas traditions\, serv
 ing as powerful symbols of hope\, renewal\, and warmth during the winter m
 onths. Their presence during the holidays\, whether through festive orname
 nts\, songs\, or sightings at feeders\, recalls ancient lore that connects
  birds with messages of love and positivity. By inviting birds into our li
 ves and honoring them through tradition\, we not only support these treasu
 red visitors but also embrace the spirit of Christmas—one of generosity\
 , harmony\, and the celebration of nature’s enduring beauty. Jim Mackie
 wicz is the owner of Wild Birds Unlimited and has been helping customers w
 ith backyard bird feeding and connecting to nature for 25 years.\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Pleasant\, Basement program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 School Age": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_school_age
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library:  Milkweed Ornament
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T160000
UID:6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Part 4 of a series of workshops for children and adults\, expl
 oring local flora through embroidery and felt collage. Hosted by Artist in
  the Library\, Sarah Zanchetta.\nAll ages\nprogram\; children ages 6 to 14
  must be accompanied by a parent or caregiver.  Registration is required. 
 Space is limited.\nNote:\nEach workshop requires separate registration. Re
 gistered participant spaces are held for 15 minutes after which drop-ins a
 re allowed\nMeet the Resident:\nSarah Zanchetta (she/they) is a queer text
 ile artist and educator whose practice dives into the loss of connection a
 nd knowledge of poisonous plants in North America. Zanchetta uses textiles
 \, texts\, and hosts community workshops to create intimate works and spac
 es where we can unfold our curiosities\, care and anxieties about what gro
 ws beside our feet. She holds a Master of Visual Studies from the Univers
 ity of Toronto (2023) and a BFA from OCAD University (2018).\nInformation 
 Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by 
 the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)
 \, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and prog
 ram registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the librar
 y handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerl
 ine@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis\, Lower Level\, Room 2
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a88d0d357c264087ad2d3a3
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — School Age
END:VEVENT
